human organs, and from the sounds made by the lower animals. {{smaller|The terms ''articulate'', ''articulation'', etc., as applied to human utterance, are not seldom misunderstood and wrongly used as if the “jointing” intended were that of the physical organs of utterance, a narrowing or closing of the organs at some point or points. Such action, however, belongs to all utterance, articulate or inarticulate, whether of man or of the other animals. See ''consonant'', ''syllable'', ''vowel''.}} Hence—'''4.''' Clear; distinct.
{{smaller block|La Fosseuse’s voice was naturally soft and low, yet ’twas an ''articulate'' voice. {{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, v. 1.}}
The sentiment of Right, once very low and indistinct, but ever more ''articulate'', because it is the voice of the universe, pronounces Freedom. {{right|''Emerson'', West Indian Emancipation, p. 175.}}}}
'''5.''' Formulated or expressed in articles, or in separate particulars. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Total changes of party and ''articulate'' opinion. {{right|''Carlyle''.}}}}
'''6†.''' Consisting of tens: as, ''articulate'' numbers.—'''Articulate adjudication.''' {{smaller|See ''adjudication''.}}
'''II.''' ''n.'' One of the ''Articulata''.

'''articulately''' (är-tik′ū-lạ̄t-li), ''adv.'' '''1.''' In a jointed manner; by joints: as, parts of a body ''articulately'' united.—'''2.''' In an articulate manner; with distinct utterance of syllables or words.
{{smaller block|Is it for nothing the wind sounds almost ''articulately'' sometimes—sings as I have lately heard it sing at night? {{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xxiv.}}}}
'''3.''' Article by article; in detail.
{{smaller block|I had ''articulately'' set down in writing our points. {{right|''Fuller'', Ch. Hist., ix. 116.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
cunning (cf. ''artifex'' (''artific-''), artist, master in any occupation), < ''ar''(''t-'')''s'', art, skill, + ''facere'', make.] '''1†.''' The art of making.
{{smaller block|Strabo affirmeth the Britons were so simple, that though they abounded in milk, they had not the ''artifice'' of cheese.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., p. 312.}}}}
'''2†.''' An ingenious or skilfully contrived work.
{{smaller block|The material universe, which is the ''artifice'' of God, the ''artifice'' of the best mechanist.
{{right|''Cudworth'', Morality, iv. 2, § 13.}}
Morality is not the ''artifice'' of ecclesiastics or politicians.
{{right|''Bibliotheca Sacra'', XLIII. 538.}}}}
'''3.''' Skill in designing and employing expedients; artful contrivance; address; trickery.
{{smaller block|His [Congreve’s] plots are constructed without much ''artifice.''
{{right|''Craik'', Hist. Eng. Lit., II. 257.}}}}
'''4.''' A crafty device; an ingenious expedient; trick; shift; piece of finesse.
{{smaller block|Those who were conscious of guilt employed numerous ''artifices'' for the purpose of averting inquiry.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xxi.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Artifice, Manœuver, Stratagem, Wile, Trick, Ruse, Finesse'', device, contrivance, cunning, craft, deception, cheat, fraud, guile, imposition, dodge, subterfuge, double-dealing. These words generally imply a careful endeavor to compass an end by deceiving others, not necessarily, however, with evil intent. They all imply management and address. An ''artifice'' is prepared with art or care; it is craftily devised. ''Manœuver'' suggests something more elaborate or intricate, a carefully contrived movement or course of action for a definite purpose; it is the quiet or secret marshaling of one’s intellectual or other resources to carry a point. ''Stratagem'' is, like ''manœuver'', a figurative term drawn from war; it is upon a larger scale what ''wile'' is upon a smaller, a device to deceive one who is the object of an imagined warfare, so that we may catch him at a disadvantage and discomfit him, or, more generally, a carefully prepared plan to carry one’s point with}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|another — to capture it or him, so to speak. A ''wile'' may be peculiarly coaxing or insinuating. ''Trick'' is the lowest and most dishonorable of these words; it may be a low or underhand act, in violation of honor or propriety, for the purpose of cheating, or something as bad. A ''ruse'' is a deception of some elaborateness, intended to cover one’s intentions, help one to escape from a predicament, etc.; it is a plausible way of bringing about what we desire to happen, without apparent interference on our part. ''Finesse'' is subtlety in action; it is a more delicate sort of ''artifice''. See ''artful'', ''evasion'', and ''fraud''.}}

{{smaller block|A favorite ''artifice'' [with Venetian beggars] is to approach Charity with a slice of polenta in one hand, and, with the other extended, implore a soldo to buy cheese to eat with the polenta. ''Howells'', Venetian Life, xx.}}

{{smaller block|Pope completely succeeded [in startling the public] by the most subtile ''manœuvres'' imaginable.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Quar. of Auth., II. 100.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>This gold must coin a ''stratagem'',
Which, cunningly effected, will beget
A very excellent piece of villainy.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., ii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Who can describe
Women’s hypocrisies! their subtle ''wiles'',
Betraying smiles, feigned tears, inconstancies!</poem>
{{right|''Otway'', Orpheus.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>But, Valentine being gone, I’ll quickly cross,
By some sly ''trick'', blunt Thurio’s dull proceeding.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., ii. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|The departure of the Mahrattas was a ''ruse''. . . . Their object in leaving the Carnatic was to blind Chunder Sahib, and in this they fully succeeded.
{{right|''J. T. Wheeler'', Short Hist. Ind., p. 237.}}}}

{{smaller block|[Montluc] was not provided with the usual means which are considered most efficient in elections, nor possessed the interest nor the splendor of his powerful competitors; he was to derive all his resources from diplomatic ''finesse''.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Curios. of Lit., IV. 261.}}}}

